description:
The function is:
```
def _coerce_stopped_terminal_reason(reason: str) -> TerminalReason:
    if reason in TERMINAL_REASON_TO_STATUS:
        return cast(TerminalReason, reason)
    return "cancelled" if reason == "cancelled" else "superseded"
```
`"cancelled"` is a key of `TERMINAL_REASON_TO_STATUS` (defined in haute.routes._job_lifecycle: the mapping has keys completed/superseded/timed_out/cancelled/memory_limited/contract_error/error). Therefore when `reason == "cancelled"`, the first `if` branch (line 383-384) already returns it and the function never reaches line 385. Conversely, line 385 is only reached when `reason not in TERMINAL_REASON_TO_STATUS`, which guarantees `reason != "cancelled"`, so the ternary's `reason == "cancelled"` test is always False and the expression unconditionally evaluates to `"superseded"`. The `"cancelled"` arm is unreachable dead code, and the conditional is misleading: it reads as if an unknown stop reason could be coerced to `cancelled`, but it can only ever become `superseded`. The intended idiom is simply `return "superseded"` — exactly mirroring the clean sibling helper `_coerce_worker_terminal_reason` in haute/routes/_background_jobs.py:231-242, which uses `if reason in {known set}: return it; return "error"` with no redundant equality re-check. No behavioral change results from replacing line 385 with `return "superseded"`.
